DAY 33 - PART A: THEORY
RBAC: Roles, ClusterRoles, Bindings, ServiceAccounts
Time: 45 minutes
==================================================================

1. AUTHENTICATION vs AUTHORIZATION
----------------------------------
Authentication (authn): WHO are you?
  - users: client certificates, OIDC tokens (company SSO), cloud IAM
    (EKS/AKS/GKE). Kubernetes has NO "User" object; a user is just a
    name inside a certificate or token.
  - ServiceAccounts: identities for PODS (and automation). These ARE
    Kubernetes objects, namespaced.
  - groups: e.g. system:masters, system:authenticated, your OIDC groups

Authorization (authz): WHAT can you do? -> RBAC.


2. RBAC = 4 OBJECTS
-------------------
  Role                permissions inside ONE namespace
  ClusterRole         permissions cluster-wide (or reusable template)
  RoleBinding         grants a Role/ClusterRole to subjects IN a namespace
  ClusterRoleBinding  grants a ClusterRole across the whole cluster

  WHO (subject)  --binding-->  WHAT (role: verbs on resources)


3. A ROLE
---------
  apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
  kind: Role
  metadata:
    name: pod-reader
    namespace: dev
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]               # "" = core group (pods, services ...)
    resources: ["pods", "pods/log"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments"]
    verbs: ["get", "list"]

Verbs: get, list, watch, create, update, patch, delete, deletecollection
Plus special ones: "pods/exec" (create), "pods/portforward", impersonate.
Find apiGroups with: k api-resources


4. A ROLEBINDING
----------------
  kind: RoleBinding
  metadata:
    name: read-pods
    namespace: dev
  subjects:
  - kind: User
    name: alice
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: viewer
    namespace: dev
  roleRef:
    kind: Role
    name: pod-reader
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

roleRef can't be changed after creation (delete + recreate).


5. BUILT-IN CLUSTERROLES
------------------------
  cluster-admin   everything
  admin           full control within a namespace (via RoleBinding)
  edit            read/write most objects, no RBAC changes
  view            read-only (no Secrets)
Bind "edit" to a team in their namespace with one RoleBinding.


6. SERVICEACCOUNTS
------------------
Every namespace has a "default" SA. Every pod runs as an SA; its token
is mounted at
  /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
Short-lived, auto-rotated (projected token). An app (CI tool, operator,
your own controller) uses it to call the API.

Good practice:
  - one SA per app; grant least privilege
  - automountServiceAccountToken: false for pods that don't need the API
  - never bind cluster-admin to an app SA
  - in clouds, SAs also map to cloud IAM roles (Week 7)

SA full name in RBAC/auth checks:
  system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>


7. TESTING PERMISSIONS
----------------------
  k auth can-i create deployments -n dev
  k auth can-i list pods -n dev --as=alice
  k auth can-i '*' '*' --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:viewer
  k auth can-i --list --as=alice -n dev


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Authn = who; RBAC = what.
- Role/ClusterRole = permissions; (Cluster)RoleBinding = who gets them.
- RBAC is additive only: no "deny" rules.
- ServiceAccounts are pod identities; least privilege.
